  "live_records": {
    "q-71250": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71250",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+z-3=0 \\\\ 3x+2y-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\times ⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
