    "q-71266": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71266",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y=0 \\\\ 4y-5z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
