    "q-129169": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129169",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3z-11=0 \\\\ 2x-2y-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j-4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
