    "q-71103": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71103",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5=0 \\\\ 2x-y+z+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
