    "q-71415": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71415",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The intersection of the planes $3x+6y+3z=0$ and $x-y+2z+3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\n3x+6y+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=-2y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z+3 & =0 \\\\ -2y-y+2(0)+3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2y=-2$ and $a=⟨-2,1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x+6y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,6,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z+3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,6,3⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =15i-3j-9k \\\\  & =⟨15,-3,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨5,-1,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-2,1,0⟩+t⟨5,-1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2+1=-1,$ and $b=⟨5,-1,-3⟩.$",
