    "q-71392": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71392",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The intersection of the planes $-x+z-1=0$ and $y+z+5=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+0-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\ny+0+5=0\\,⟹\\,y=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨-1,-5,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+z-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+z+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-5,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1+(-5)=-6,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,-1⟩.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-71392-a",
