    "q-71467": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71467",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Given the two planes $3x-6y+1=0$ and $-x+2y=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nIII. The planes intersect in a straight line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that:  \n\n- For the plane $3x-6y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-6,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x+2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-6,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i+0j+0k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}=0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are parallel. However,  the second plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the first plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n3(0)-6(0)+1=1\\ne 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
