    "q-71456": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71456",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Given the two planes $x-3y+z=0$ and $2x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,5,2⟩.$\n\nII. The planes intersect on a line.\n\nIII. The planes are coincident.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-3,1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+2j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,5⟩\\ne ⟨1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
